          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The theory of hyperfunctions, created by the Japanese school of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikio+Sato"} Mikio Sato
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Masaki+Kashiwara"} Masaki Kashiwara
            et al. is one of the many variants of the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+functions"} generalized functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            of Schwarz et alt. it is not based on duals of some spaces of smooth functions but rather on boundary values of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+functions"} holomorphic functions
          %p
            Hyperfunctions are a very useful tool in the study of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-modules"} D-modules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/harmonic+analysis"} harmonic analysis
            that are part of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+analysis"} microlocal analysis
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+microlocalization"} algebraic microlocalization
          %p
            This page is about hyperfunctions of one variable only, for the multiple variable case see
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                hyperfunction of multiple variables
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/hyperfunction+of+multiple+variables"} ?
          %h2#abstract Abstract
          %p We define hyperfunctions and explain some basic properties.
          %p
            The theory of hyperfunctions of one variable is considerably easier than the one for multiple variables, for this reason we will split the exposition to handle the one dimensional case first, and then generalize to multiple dimensions:
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                hyperfunction of multiple variables
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/hyperfunction+of+multiple+variables"} ?
          %p The exposition of the one dimensional theory will try to illuminate the following points:
          %ol
            %li
              Hyperfunctions are more general than
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperfunction#Hyerfunction_Not_Distribution"} example
          %p A basic strategy of the exposition will therefore be to stress both similarities and differences of hyperfunctions and distributions (real analytic and smooth setting).
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Hyperfunctions require less technical machinery, at least in one dimension, since they can be defined and studied with basic complex analysis only. That will nevertheless make it possible to give a rigorous definitions of otherwise only formal expressions that are often used by engineers and physicists, like
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mo ∫
                          %mi a
                          %mi b
                        %mi δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi dx
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a^b \delta (x) dx
            %li
              %p
                Hyperfunctions and their
                = succeed "s" do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    microfunction
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/microfunction"} ?
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flabby+sheaves"} flabby sheaves
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+analysis"} algebraic analysis
                of systems of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equations"} differential equations
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = (a, b) \subseteq \mathbb{R}
            be an open interval, a complex neighbourhood of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is an open set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset \mathbb{C}
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo =
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \bigcap \mathbb{R} = I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \subset \mathbb{C}
            open let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}(V)
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            of holomorphic functions on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          .un_def
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              The set of hyperfunctions on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              for a complex neighbourhood
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is defined to be the quotient
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo ∖
                        %mi I
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{B}(I) \coloneqq \frac{ \mathcal{O}(U \setminus I) }{\mathcal{O}(U)}
          %p
            The set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}(I)
            does not depend on the chosen complex neighbourhood
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %span.newWikiWord
              Mittag-Leffler's theorem
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Mittag-Leffler%27s+theorem"} ?
            in the one-dimensional case. The definition easily generalizes to open subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mo +
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Im
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^+ \coloneqq \{u \in U : \operatorname{Im}(u) \gt 0 \}
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mo −
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Im
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^- \coloneqq \{u \in U : \operatorname{Im}(u) \lt 0 \}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^+
            holomorphic on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^+
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^-
            holomorphic on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^-
            such that there is no function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            holomorphic on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            that restricts to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^+
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^+
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^-
          %p
            In the following we will often write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = (F^+, F^-)
            for a hyperfunction
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F^+, F^-)
            being a representative of the equivalence class that is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            on some complex neighbourhood. If the algebraic expression of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^+
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^-
            coincides we can further simplify the notation and simply write
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [F]
          %h3#examples Examples
          %p
            The
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            distribution can be represented by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi π
                        %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta = [\frac{i}{2 \pi z}]
          %p
            The Heaviside function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(x)
            has as a representation:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                  %mi log
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H(x) = [- \frac{1}{2 \pi i} log(-z)]
          %p
            Here we use the main branch of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logarithm"} logarithm
            that is defined on the complex plane minus the negative real axis, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi log
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} log(-z)
            takes the value
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi log
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi π
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} log(|z|) - \pi \, i
            on the upper side of the positive real axis and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi log
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi π
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} log(|z|) + \pi \, i
            on the lower side of the positive real axis, while being holomorphic on the negative real axis. This results in
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                      %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi i
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %mi i
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "left left", :displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                          %mtd
                            %mtext if
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi x
                            %mo &gt;
                            %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ,
                          %mtd
                            %mtext if
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi x
                            %mo &lt;
                            %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H(x) = \lim_{\epsilon \to 0+} (F^+ (x + i \epsilon) - F^- (x - i \epsilon)) =
                  
                  \\begin{cases}
                  
                  1, &amp; \text{if}\; x \gt 0  \\
                  0, &amp; \text{if}\; x \lt 0
                  
                  \\end{cases}
          %p
            and a singularity in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=0
          %h2#Basic_Properties_One_Variable Basic Properties and Definitions
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq \mathbb{R}
            be open.
          %h3#derivative Derivative
          .un_def
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong derivative
              of a hyperfunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = [F^+, F^-]
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi dF
                          %mo +
                      %mi dz
                    %mo ,
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi dF
                          %mo −
                      %mi dz
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F' = [\frac{dF^+}{dz}, \frac{dF^-}{dz}]
          %p We immediatly see that the derivative of a hyperfunction is again a hyperfunction and that all hyperfunctions are indefinitly differentiable.
          %h4#examples_2 Examples
          %p As the derivative of the Heaviside function we obtain:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mi dz
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                  %mi log
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi i
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H'(x) = [\frac{d}{dz} (- \frac{1}{2 \pi i} log(-z))] = [- \frac{1}{2 \pi \, i\, z}] = \delta (x)
          %p
            Note that we can derive this relationship without any resort to dualities of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TVS"} TVS
          %h3#sheaf_structure Sheaf Structure
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (hyperfunctions are a module over analytic functions)
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}(U)
              be the algebra of real analytic functions on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}(U)
              is a module over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}(U)
          %p
            In fact every real analytic function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is naturally an element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}(U)
            represented by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [f, 0]
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, -f]
            on a complex neighbourhood. Given any hyperfunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = (F^+, F^-)
            we can define the product by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi f
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f F := (f F^+, F^-) = (F^+, -f F^-)
            which can be shown to be independent of the various choices involved (representation of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (hyperfunctions form a
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flabby+sheaf"} flabby sheaf
              The sets of hyperfunctions of open subsets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              form a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flabby+sheaf"} flabby sheaf
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (distributions are a subsheaf)
              There is a linear injection from the space of distributions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}'(U)
              to the space of hyperfunctions
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}(U)
          %h3#multiplication Multiplication
          %p There is no product of hyperfunctions that reduces to the ordinary product for real analytic functions and respects the chain rule of differential calculus, just as in the case of distributions. Nevertheless certain hyperfunctions may be multiplied, more on that later.
          %p
            First let us note that the naive definition of the product of two hyperfunctions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = [F^+, F^-], G = [G^+, G^-]
            via
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FG
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  FG := [F^+ G^+, F^- G^-]
          %p is obviously not well defined on equivalence classes. …
          %h3#support Support
          %p
            In general we cannot speak of the value of a hyperfunction at a certain point, much like we cannot speak of the value of a distribution at a certain point. We say that a hyperfunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            vanishes on an open subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U'
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            if it coincides with the zero hyperfunction, which is of course equivalent to stating that there is a representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = (F^+, F^-)
            such that the boundary values of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^+
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^-
            coincide on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U'
            and therefore define a real analytic function on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U'
          .un_def
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong support
              of a hyperfunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is the complement of the largest open subset of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              on which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              vanishes in the sense described above.
          %p
            For any compact
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            we will denote by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}_K
            all hyperfunctions whose support is contained in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %h4#integration_for_compactly_supported_hyperfunctions Integration for Compactly Supported Hyperfunctions
          %p
            Since we defined hyperfunctions for open subsets only, we do not yet know anything about hyperfunctions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}_K
            with support in a compact subset
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_4 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (characterization of compactly supported hyperfunctions)
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}_K
              be all hyperfunctions with support contained in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \subset \mathbb{R}
              compact and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              be any complex neighbourhood of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi K
                    %mo ≅
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo ∖
                        %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{B}_K \cong \frac{ \mathcal{O}(V\setminus K) }{\mathcal{O}(V)}
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in \mathbb{R}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq b
            and suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a hyperfunction that is analytical in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a, b]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi τ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau^+
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^+
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi τ
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau^-
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^-
            from a to b and a representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = (F^+, F^-)
            such that we can define the integral
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi dx
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi τ
                        %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi dz
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi τ
                        %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi dz
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_a^b F(x) dx := \int_{\tau^+} F^+ (z) dz - \int_{\tau^-} F^- (z) dz
          %p independently of all the arbitrary choices we made, thanks to the holomorphy of the functions on the right side (and Cauchy’s integral formula for holomorphic functions, of course).
          %h4#example_of_integration_for_hyperfunctions Example of Integration for Hyperfunctions
          %p Using the definition of an integral of a hyperfunction above, we can easily prove
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                  %mi F
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi dx
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_a^b F'(x) dx = F(b) - F(b)
          %p
            for all hyperfunctions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            that are analytic in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi dx
                  %mo =
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_a^b \delta (x) dx = H(b) - H(a)
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \neq 0
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            the Heaviside function.
          %h3#Hyerfunction_Not_Distribution Example of a Hyperfunction that is not a Distribution
          %p Warning: As of this moment this paragraph consists of some handwaving.
          %p
            A well known structure theorem in the theory of distributions says that every distribution whose support consists of one single point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is in fact a finite linear combination of distributional derivatives of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            function at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p
            As an example of a hyperfunction that is not a distribution we therefore have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi exp
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = [\exp{\frac{1}{z}}]
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} supp
                    %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \supp{F} = \{ 0 \}
          %p
            Using the representation of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            distribution, we see that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mi n
                      %mo !
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta^{(n)}(x) = [\frac{(-1)^{n+1} n!}{2 \pi i} \frac{1}{z^{n+1}}]
          %p
            Using this representation, for any real analytic test function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            we see that the following identity holds, which motivates the use of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            as in distribution theory:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi dx
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int a(x) \delta^{n}(x) dx = a^{(n)} (0)
          %p
            Comparing the Laurent series of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            with the representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta^{(n)}
            we get
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi exp
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mi i
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %mn ∞
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\exp{\frac{1}{z}}] = [1 + 2 \pi i  \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}  \frac{1}{n! (n-1)!} \delta^{(n)}(z)]
          %p
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo !
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)! := 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi exp
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\exp{\frac{1}{z}}]
            cannot be a distribution. There is an analog structure theorem for hyperfunctions, however:
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_5 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (structure of hyperfunctions supported at a single point)
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              be a hyperfunction supported at the origin, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              has a representation as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mi δ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F(x) = [ \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} b_n \delta^{(n)} (x)]
            %p where the coefficients satisfy
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %mn ∞
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %msub
                          %mi b
                          %mi n
                        %mi n
                        %mo !
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\lim_{n \to \infty} \sqrt{|b_n n!|} = 0
          %p We include a sketch of the proof of the first statement to illustrate the ease of the use of hyperfunctions.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              be a hyperfunction supported at the origin, that means that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is analytic in a complex neighbourhood of the origin (excluding the origin itself). Therefore
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              has a representation by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laurent+series"} Laurent series
              in a neighbourhood of the origin:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn ∞
                      %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn ∞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F(x) = [\sum_{- \infty}^{\infty} a_n z^n] = [\sum_{- \infty}^{-1} a_n z^n]
            %p
              In the representation of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              we may remove the holomorphic part of the Laurent series. Now we may insert the representation of the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
              hyperfunctions and get
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn ∞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mi δ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn ∞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi δ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F(x) = [\sum_{- \infty}^{-1} b_n \delta^{(n)} (z)] = \sum_{- \infty}^{-1} b_n [\delta^{(n)} (z)]
            %p The last sum is understood to converge in the topology of uniform convergence on compact subsets where the summands are holomorph.
          %h3#topology_and_duality_for_compactly_supported_hyperfunctions Topology and Duality for Compactly Supported Hyperfunctions
          %p
            We can introduce a canonical topology on the space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}_K
            of hyperfunctions with support in a compact
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            and show that the topological dual is the space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}_K
            of germs of real analytical functions, that is the inductive limit of the spaces of holomorphic functions on a cofinal set of complex neighbourhoods of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %p
            The algebraic statement is known as
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Köthe’s (duality) theorem
          %p
            This will also hint at the fact that we will not be able to introduce a topology on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R})
            that resembles the situation in the theory of distributions, since its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+vector+space"} dual space
            would be something like “the space of compactly supported real analytic functions”.
          %p …
          %h2#main_theoretical_results Main Theoretical Results
          %p One striking example of the use of hyperfunctions in the theory of differential equations with real analytic coefficients is this result due to Sato:
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq \mathbb{R}
            be open and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            be a linear, finite order differential operator with real analytic coefficients defined on
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mi dx
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %mi n
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mi dx
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P(x, \frac{d}{dx}) = \sum_{i=0}^n a(x) (\frac{d}{dx})^{i}
          %p
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_n \neq 0
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_6 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (solvability of differential equations)
              For every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in \mathcal{B}(U)
              there is a solution
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in \mathcal{B}(U)
              of the equation
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi u
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P u = f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊃
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \supset U
              iff the coefficients
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              can be extended to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Briefly:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is surjective on hyperfunctions.
          %p Two related results are:
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_7 Theorem
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              is a sheaf endomorphismus, both of the sheaf of real analytic functions and of the sheaf of hyperfunctions.
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_8 Theorem
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              does not enlarge the support of hyperfunctions.
          %h2#singularities_and_microfunctions Singularities and Microfunctions
          %p Much information about a given hyperfunction is encoded in the kind of singularities that it has. A first step into the theory of singularities is the following definition:
          .un_def
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong
                singular support sing supp(
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                )
              of a hyperfunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is the complement of the largest open set on which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is real analytic.
          %p
            This definition does not seem particularly useful, since it only pinpoints the singular locus of a hyperfunction, without explaining the differences between various singularities. One question of crucial importance to physics is for example “when can two
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
          %p
            The singular support does not help much: The
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            distribution cannot be squared, while the distribution defined by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\frac{1}{x + \epsilon i}]
            can. (The latter is a distribution defined as a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+principal+value"} Cauchy principal value
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            distribution is worse than that of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo +
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\frac{1}{x + \epsilon i}]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefront+sets"} wavefront sets
            in the smooth setting. Note that the definition of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefront+set"} wavefront set
            needs the concept of a cutoff function, that is a smooth function with compact support, which cannot be used in the real analytic setting.
          %p …
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+analysis"}> microlocal analysis
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"} wave front set
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              Springer Online Enc. of Math.:
              %a{:href => "http://eom.springer.de/h/h048420.htm"} hyperfunction
          %p A gentle introduction with examples is the booklet
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                K. Yosida,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Operational calculus. A theory of hyperfunctions
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikio+Sato"}> Mikio Sato
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Theory of hyperfunctions I
                %a{:href => "https://repository.dl.itc.u-tokyo.ac.jp/?action=repository_uri&item_id=39927&file_id=19&file_no=1"} pdf scan
            %li
              %p
                M.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kashiwara"} Kashiwara
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Foundation of algebraic analysis
            %li
              %p
                Mitsuo Morimoto:
                %em An introduction to Sato’s hyperfunctions.
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:0811.46034&format=complete"}> ZMATH entry
                )
            %li
              %p
                A. Eida, S. Pilipović,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the microlocal decomposition of same classes of hyperfunctions
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 125
          %p It is possible to use hyperfunctions as an introduction to generalized functions for physicists and engineers with a minimal background in complex analysis, see
          %ul
            %li
              Urs Graf:
              %em Introduction to hyperfunctions and their integral transforms
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:pre05662238&format=complete"} ZMATH entry
              %a{:href => "https://docs.google.com/viewer?a=v&pid=explorer&chrome=true&srcid=0B_dBawvWxGGQODUyNDhjNzktNjM3Mi00NzM0LTg4ZDYtMWIzNGIwNWZkMzdi&hl=en_US"} pdf
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/analysis"} analysis
